CPU SCHEDULING IN OPERATING SYSTEM

CPU Scheduling is the process by which the operating system selects one process from the ready queue and allocates the CPU to it. Since multiple processes may be ready to execute at the same time, the operating system must decide which process gets CPU time.

CPU scheduling improves system efficiency, ensures fairness, and maximizes CPU utilization.

Need for CPU Scheduling

In a multiprogramming system, many processes are loaded into memory at the same time. Only one process can use the CPU at a time. Therefore, the operating system must decide which process should run next. This decision-making mechanism is called CPU scheduling.

The main goals of CPU scheduling are:

Maximize CPU utilization
Minimize waiting time
Minimize turnaround time
Minimize response time
Provide fairness to all processes

Basic Scheduling Terms

Arrival Time
The time at which a process enters the ready queue.

Burst Time
The amount of time a process needs on the CPU.

Completion Time
The time at which a process finishes execution.

Turnaround Time
Completion Time minus Arrival Time.

Waiting Time
Turnaround Time minus Burst Time.

Response Time
The time between submission of request and first response.

Types of CPU Scheduling Algorithms

First Come First Serve (FCFS)

In FCFS scheduling, the process that arrives first is executed first. It follows the principle of queue. It is simple to implement but may cause long waiting time for short processes.

Advantages
Simple and easy to implement
No starvation

Disadvantages
High average waiting time
Convoy effect may occur

Shortest Job First (SJF)

In SJF scheduling, the process with the smallest burst time is executed first. It can be preemptive or non-preemptive.

Advantages
Minimum average waiting time
Efficient for batch systems

Disadvantages
Difficult to predict burst time
Possible starvation of long processes

Priority Scheduling

In Priority Scheduling, each process is assigned a priority. The process with the highest priority is executed first. It can also be preemptive or non-preemptive.

Advantages
Important processes are executed first

Disadvantages
Low priority processes may suffer starvation

Round Robin Scheduling

Round Robin scheduling assigns a fixed time quantum to each process. Each process gets CPU for a limited time. If it does not complete within that time, it goes back to the ready queue.

Advantages
Fair allocation of CPU
Good for time-sharing systems

Disadvantages
Performance depends on time quantum size

Preemptive vs Non-Preemptive Scheduling

Preemptive Scheduling
The operating system can interrupt a running process and allocate CPU to another process.

Non-Preemptive Scheduling
Once a process starts execution, it runs until it finishes or enters waiting state.

Conclusion

CPU Scheduling is a fundamental concept in operating systems. It ensures efficient utilization of CPU and smooth execution of multiple processes. Different scheduling algorithms are used based on system requirements such as fairness, efficiency, and response time. Understanding CPU scheduling is essential for exams, interviews, and system design knowledge.
